id: rule-consequence-known-triple
title: Using a known triple
topic: Consequence
example-of: CONSEQUENCE
derived: true

task:
  We know the triple $\{x >= 0\}$ `y := x + 1` $\{y >= 1\}$. Use the
  consequence rule to derive from it the triple below. The known triple
  gives $P'$ and $Q'$.

given:
  Known triple: $\{x >= 0\}$ `y := x + 1` $\{y >= 1\}$

pre: x = 3
post: y > 0

scheme: CONSEQUENCE
  First premise: «P» ==> «P'»
  Middle premise: {«P'»} y := x + 1 {«Q'»}
  Last premise: «Q'» ==> «Q»
  ---
  {«P»} y := x + 1 {«Q»}

program:
  {P}
  {P'}
  y := x + 1     // i1
  {Q'}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The known triple
part: Middle premise
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i1
text:
  The middle premise is the known triple. We check it with the rule
  ASSIGN: we substitute the variable $y$ with $x + 1$ in $Q'$.
set: Q' = y >= 1
set: P' = x >= 0
calc:
  wp(y := x + 1, Q')
  = (y >= 1)[y <- x + 1]     // Q' is y >= 1
  = x + 1 >= 1               // y replaced by x + 1
  = x >= 0                   // subtract 1 from both sides
text:
  The result is $P'$, so the rule ASSIGN gives the known triple.

step: P implies P'
part: First premise
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, P'
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «P'»
vc: x = 3 ==> x >= 0
why:
  When $x = 3$, $x >= 0$ is true.

step: Q' implies Q
part: Last premise
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: Q', Q
text:
  >>> «Q'» ==> «Q»
vc: y >= 1 ==> y > 0
why:
  For integers, $y >= 1$ is the same as $y > 0$. All three premises hold:
  the consequence rule strengthens the precondition and weakens the
  postcondition of the known triple.
